{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b P2. The two human layers\par}
{\pard\sa160 The paper and the appendix are written by the authors, with whatever help they choose. Headings that a claim points at carry an explicit id, {\f1 ### Proposition 1 \{#prop-1\}}. The appendix ends with a section {\f1 ## Scope and choices \{#scope-and-choices\}} holding two subsections:\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab {\f1 ### Choices \{#choices\}}: the choices made and why; what was tried and set aside.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab {\f1 ### Does not claim \{#does-not-claim\}}: what the paper does not claim; which frameworks are not to be imported.\par}
{\pard\sa160 These are the authors' own words; the AI appendix carries them as parts 4 and 6 (P3).\par}
{\pard\sa160 The paper opens with a front notice (added in formal-0.3). Before its first section, after the title and byline, a paragraph beginning {\f1 **AI appendix.**} says that an AI appendix in this protocol's form accompanies the paper and where it is: {\f1 ai-appendix/} in the record, and the published location once the paper is published. Level 1 checks that the notice is there; its wording beyond those two facts is the authors'. The notice states facts and addresses no reader: like any text in the record, a sentence addressed to an evaluator is reported under P12.\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b P3. The AI appendix\par}
{\pard\sa160 The author's statement to the machine: how the paper is to be read by the reader that evaluates. Its content is the author's alone; the journal publishes the form and requires conformance to it at submission. Six parts:\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 1.\tab {\f1 definitions.md}: definitions of record, every term used in a non-default sense.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 2.\tab {\f1 procedure.md}: the solution method, step by step.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 3.\tab {\f1 claims.yaml}: the claims list (P4).\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 4.\tab {\f1 stance.md}: the authors' stance, the words of {\f1 #choices}.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 5.\tab {\f1 anchors.yaml}: reproduction anchors (P5).\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 6.\tab {\f1 scope.md}: scope and do-nots, the words of {\f1 #does-not-claim}.\par}
{\pard\sa160 Declarations live once, in {\f1 declarations.yaml}, rather than as a seventh part. The author may write the AI appendix with any tool, or by hand, and may edit it. The journal's generator (M0) is the reference implementation of this form, offered to authors as a convenience; a submission that arrives without an AI appendix receives one from it on intake, and the report says so. An AI appendix the generator wrote carries a stamp, {\f1 generated.yaml}: the generator version, the run that produced it, and the hash of every input and output (schema {\f1 schemas/generated.schema.yaml}). Whether the AI appendix still matches its stamp is recorded on the findings report as a fact; it is not a finding (formal-0.3; formal-0.2 had check (d)). What the AI appendix says is held to the paper, the appendix and the record by the Level 2 checks (P10): understatement by completeness, overstatement by resolution.\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b P4. The claims list\par}
{\pard\sa160 Every claim the contribution rests on, schema {\f1 schemas/claims.schema.yaml}. Each claim has an id ({\f1 C1}, ...), a kind ({\f1 result}, {\f1 causal}, {\f1 contribution}), a form for formal results ({\f1 proposition}, {\f1 comparative_static}, {\f1 lemma}, {\f1 example}), a {\f1 main} flag, the text, the conditions it is stated under, a pointer to where the paper states it, a pointer to the evidence in the record, and the derivation that supports it. The list contains at least one contribution claim and at least one main claim. The contribution claim also carries the question it answers, in the paper's words, and a pointer to where the paper states it (formal-0.4): the evidence map places the claim on that question, not on a reading of the machine's own.\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b P5. Reproduction anchors\par}
{\pard\sa160 Three to five results a reader must reproduce before proceeding, schema {\f1 schemas/anchors.schema.yaml}. An anchor re-runs a function in {\f1 materials/} with stated arguments, or evaluates an ASCII expression in the symbols of a named derivation, and compares the result with the stated value: a number within a tolerance, a sign, or an identity.\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b P6. Provenance: the derivation behind each proposition\par}
{\pard\sa160 For formal work, provenance means the derivation behind each proposition, stated checkably in {\f1 provenance/derivations.yaml} (schema {\f1 schemas/derivations.schema.yaml}): the symbols and their domains and meanings, the assumptions, the objective and the choice variable, the stated solution, the derived quantities, the closed forms stated in the paper, the comparative statics with their stated signs, and the numerical examples. Every proposition, comparative static and lemma in the claims list names a derivation. Expressions are ASCII arithmetic in declared symbols; nothing else is read.\par}
{\pard\sa160 A result that is proved rather than solved, a theorem with a proof, is a derivation of kind {\f1 proof} (added in formal-0.2): the hypotheses as the paper states them, the steps of the argument, and for each step what it rests on, as pointers into the record (P9) or as {\f1 cited: ...} for work outside it, and the conclusion. The machine checks that the pointers resolve and that nothing rests on itself; it does not judge whether a step follows. Numerical facts a theorem-paper states, worked examples and thresholds, go in closed-form derivations beside it.\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b P7. Declarations and the AI-use record\par}
{\pard\sa160 {\f1 declarations.yaml} states the modality, the version of the standard followed, the access tier of the materials, how AI was used (tools and roles), and whether the working history is submitted. {\f1 provenance/ai-use.yaml} is the AI-use record: one entry per use, with the tool, the role, what it touched (a pointer into the record), the date, and the human direction behind it. Every tool the declarations name has an entry. When the generator produced the AI appendix, the record names that run, and it must match the stamp's run; whether the files still match the stamp is a fact on the report (P3). The record cannot prove human direction; like an authorship statement, it rests on good faith and reputation (appendix B.6).\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b P8. Submission\par}
{\pard\sa160 The author submits the record together with their own findings report from the published checks, run on this exact version of the record with the published versions. The report is a declaration of participation and the baseline the journal's run is compared with; it is not proof of a pass.\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b P9. Pointer grammar\par}
